# A186 — a nested filename in a checkpoint's list (2026-09-15T15:41:22Z)

## 1. before: the target directory was never made
$ git show HEAD:tools/install.sh | grep -n "local target=" -B 2
  274-  local name="$1" expected="$2"
  275-  local url="https://huggingface.co/$MODEL_ID/resolve/main/$name"
  276:  local target="$MODEL_DIR/$name"
$ git show HEAD:tools/install.sh | grep -n "mkdir -p" | head -2
  194:mkdir -p "$MODELS_DIR"
  196:mkdir -p "$MODEL_DIR"
  (only the model directory is created; the listed name is used verbatim)

$ curl -fsSL -o /tmp/a186/model/original/config.json http://127.0.0.1:8731/x
  exit=23
  curl: (23) Failure writing output to destination, passed 8 returned 4294967295
  — a reachable source, so the only thing left that can fail is where the bytes are meant to go:
    curl -o cannot create the directory, download_file returned non-zero, and the install counted a
    failed download. The first attempt at this measurement used an unreachable host and failed with
    a connection error instead, which would have been evidence of nothing — recorded because a wrong
    demonstration is a likelier mistake than a wrong fix.

## 2. after: the path helper makes the directory, and refuses anything that leaves it
$ grep -n "model-target-path" tools/install.sh
  280:  if ! target="$(bash "$SCRIPT_DIR/model-target-path.sh" "$MODEL_DIR" "$name" 2>&1)"; then

  config.json              -> /tmp/a186/model/config.json
  original/config.json     -> /tmp/a186/model/original/config.json
  a/b/c.json               -> /tmp/a186/model/a/b/c.json
  ../../etc/passwd         -> refusing '../../etc/passwd': it is not a plain path inside the checkpoint
  /etc/passwd              -> refusing '/etc/passwd': it is not a plain path inside the checkpoint

  the directories it made:
    .
    ./a
    ./a/b
    ./original

$ shellcheck tools/install.sh tools/model-target-path.sh
  clean

  Limit, stated: the installer itself is not run end to end — it downloads about 3 GB — so what is
  exercised here is the path rule it now calls, and the call site is checked by shellcheck and the
  script's own bash -n.
